// # aarch64 contract coverage — feature-by-feature audit
//
// Logline: Map every top-level language feature (let, fn, match, if, while, for, struct, list, opt, int, float, string, fn call, return, rec fn, mut local) to the aarch64 contract gate's coverage, the existence of an aarch64_emit_<feature>.oo module, and the empty-on-shape-mismatch policy, then rank the weakest and compare against the x86 row.
//
// Setup: aarch64_emit_contract.oo at ooda/oodac/aarch64_emit_contract.oo (225 lines) is the contract gate. The gate walks tokens for KW_FN, KW_REQUIRES, KW_ENSURES, KW_VERIFY, and IDENT "spec" only (lines 84, 91, 107, 120). No other language features are directly handled. The aarch64 path is harvest-driven (aarch64_emit.oo:22-127) and lacks the x86_emit_<feature>.oo file family that gives the x86 audit its 2/3 column. The empty-on-shape-mismatch policy appears in aarch64_emit_take.oo:68, 165, aarch64_emit_harv.oo:91, and aarch64_emit_contract.oo:155, 168, 187, 221; aarch64_emit_if.oo uses a position-return-on-mismatch pattern instead.
//
// Beats:
//   1. Build a 16-row coverage matrix of features × (contract, emit, empty).
//   2. List features with 0/3 coverage (every cell N).
//   3. List features with 1/3 coverage.
//   4. Compare aarch64 against the x86 row (better, worse, same).
//   5. Propose one contract-gate addition per uncovered feature.
//   6. Rank the top 3 weakest features with a concrete action plan.
//
// residual: Tier 0 #2 (secret) and Tier 0 #3 (contract) are in flight; aarch64_emit_secret.oo exists but aarch64_emit_diag_test returns empty until shape-gate rules are added (aarch64_emit_diag.oo:12, 154-161). Tier 5 typed-AST may subsume the token-walker chain.

File: openOODA/audit/aarch64-contract-coverage.oot
Status: open — Tier 0 contract-gate follow-up; aarch64 coverage is shallower than x86.
Context: Read-only audit. No .oo files were modified.

---

## 1. Coverage matrix

The contract gate at ooda/oodac/aarch64_emit_contract.oo only processes KW_FN, KW_REQUIRES, KW_ENSURES, KW_VERIFY, and IDENT "spec". Every other language feature is not directly handled. The aarch64 path is harvest-driven and does not have a literal aarch64_emit_<feature>.oo family; features are folded into aarch64_emit_take.oo (let, fn call 0-arg, minus-IDENT call, return-string scan), aarch64_emit_if.oo (if), aarch64_emit_num.oo (int), aarch64_emit_harv.oo (string fold), and aarch64_emit_fold.oo (frame). The "empty" column asks whether the closest emit function declares `let empty: List[String] = list_new()` and returns it on shape mismatch.

| #  | Feature    | Contract         | Emit file                                       | Empty | Evidence                                       |
|----|------------|------------------|-------------------------------------------------|-------|------------------------------------------------|
| 1  | let binding| N                | N (closest: aarch64_emit_take.oo)              | Y/N*  | aarch64_emit_take.oo:68, 73, 161               |
| 2  | fn decl    | Y (KW_FN tracked)| N                                               | N/A   | aarch64_emit_contract.oo:84-89                 |
| 3  | match      | N                | N                                               | N/A   | (no file)                                      |
| 4  | if         | N                | Y (aarch64_emit_if.oo)                          | N     | aarch64_emit_if.oo:16-22, 43-63                |
| 5  | while      | N                | N                                               | N/A   | (no file)                                      |
| 6  | for        | N                | N                                               | N/A   | (no file)                                      |
| 7  | struct     | N                | N                                               | N/A   | (no file)                                      |
| 8  | list       | N                | N                                               | N/A   | (no file)                                      |
| 9  | opt        | N                | N                                               | N/A   | (no file)                                      |
| 10 | int        | N                | N (closest: aarch64_emit_num.oo)               | N/A   | aarch64_emit_num.oo:14-26                      |
| 11 | float      | N                | N                                               | N/A   | (no file)                                      |
| 12 | string     | N                | N (closest: aarch64_emit_harv.oo)              | N/A   | aarch64_emit_harv.oo:74-88                     |
| 13 | fn call    | N                | N (0-arg only, in aarch64_emit_take.oo)         | N/A   | aarch64_emit_take.oo:110-129, 218-231          |
| 14 | return     | N                | N (closest: aarch64_emit_take.oo:17, 36)       | N/A   | aarch64_emit_take.oo:17-34, 36-65              |
| 15 | rec fn     | N                | N                                               | N/A   | (no file)                                      |
| 16 | mut local  | N                | N                                               | N/A   | (no file — KW_MUT not in aarch64_emit_take)    |

Totals: 16 features surveyed. 0 with 3/3. 0 with 2/3. 2 with 1/3. 14 with 0/3.

Notes on the *let-binding* "Y/N*": a64_record_let (aarch64_emit_take.oo:67) declares `let empty: List[String] = list_new()` and returns it for untyped let, multi-binding let, and let with a struct value, so the empty policy is Y for the let matcher. But the function lives in aarch64_emit_take.oo, not aarch64_emit_let.oo, so the literal emit column is N. Treat as 1/3 (empty) when the closest file is counted, or 0/3 when the literal-name rule is enforced. The x86 audit uses the literal-name rule; we follow it.

Notes on the fn-decl "Y": same caveat as the x86 audit (audit/x86-contract-coverage.oot:47). The contract gate touches KW_FN at aarch64_emit_contract.oo:84 only to track fn_name so the next requires/ensures/spec/verify clause can be attributed. The fn body, parameters, and return type are not validated. The aarch64 path has no aarch64_emit_fn.oo to do the second job.

---

## 2. Features with 0/3 coverage (every cell N)

Fourteen features have no contract coverage, no literal aarch64_emit_<feature>.oo file, and (by extension) no first-class empty-return policy:

1. **let binding** — closest is a64_record_let in aarch64_emit_take.oo:67-162. The matcher accepts string, int (with optional ALU), int-minus, bool, and 0-arg/1-arg fn-call lets only. Untyped let, multi-binding let, let with struct value, and let in a nested scope have no contract check; the empty policy is Y in a64_record_let but the file is not named for "let".
2. **match** — no file. The harvest walker in aarch64_emit.oo:22-127 never enters a match arm; KW_MATCH is not scanned in aarch64_emit_contract.oo. Every match expression silently emits nothing.
3. **while** — no file. The harvest walker tracks `let mut i: Int = N` only as a let-binding (aarch64_emit.oo:88-99). KW_WHILE never appears in aarch64_emit_contract.oo. Loops are out of scope for the harvest.
4. **for** — no file. KW_FOR not in aarch64_emit_contract.oo. No matcher; every for-loop is silently dropped.
5. **struct** — no file. KW_STRUCT not in aarch64_emit_contract.oo. Structs cannot be constructed today; the audit's `let s: S = ...` row from x86 has no aarch64 equivalent.
6. **list** — no file. KW_LIST not in aarch64_emit_contract.oo. List literals are silently dropped.
7. **opt** — no file. KW_OPT not in aarch64_emit_contract.oo. Optional bindings are out of scope.
8. **int** — no aarch64_emit_int.oo. Closest is aarch64_emit_num.oo:14-26 (a64_is_cmp, a64_is_alu) and aarch64_emit_num.oo:163-186 (a64_alu_i). These are if-cond helpers, not int-typed-literal lowerers. The contract gate does not look for KW_INT.
9. **float** — no file. KW_FLOAT not in aarch64_emit_contract.oo. No float matcher; any float literal is silently dropped.
10. **string** — no aarch64_emit_str.oo. Closest is a64_str_fold in aarch64_emit_harv.oo:74-88 and a64_mix_try in aarch64_emit_harv.oo:90-136. These fold STRING plus STRING chains but do not validate let-binding shapes. Contract gate does not look for STRING.
11. **fn call** — no single aarch64 file. a64_record_let in aarch64_emit_take.oo:110-129 handles 0-arg fn calls and 1-arg STRING calls. a64_take_println in aarch64_emit_take.oo:218-231 handles minus-IDENT 0-arg calls. A 2+arg call, a call with a non-int/non-string arg, a call with a list/struct arg, or a call that returns Int has no matcher and no contract check.
12. **return** — closest is a64_fn_ret_str in aarch64_emit_take.oo:17-34 and a64_fn_call_str in aarch64_emit_take.oo:36-65. Both only fold a single `return STRING` shape inside a callee; multi-line returns, returns of int, and returns of bare IDENT have no matcher. The contract gate does not look at KW_RETURN.
13. **rec fn** — no file. KW_FN with a recursive call inside is not detected by aarch64_emit_contract.oo. The aarch64 path has no aarch64_emit_rec.oo equivalent of x86_emit_rec.oo.
14. **mut local** — no file. KW_MUT is not in the token walker's branch set (aarch64_emit.oo:88 looks for KW_LET but never inspects KW_MUT). Let-bindings are recorded into bnames/bvals (strings) or inames/ivals (ints) without a mut flag, so the aarch64 path silently treats mut and immutable locals identically.

---

## 3. Features with 1/3 coverage

Two features have exactly one of the three cells as Y:

1. **fn decl** (1/3) — contract Y (KW_FN tracked at aarch64_emit_contract.oo:84), emit N (no aarch64_emit_fn.oo), empty N/A. Same caveat as the x86 row: the contract gate uses fn_name as a context anchor for the next clause, not as a real fn-decl validator.
2. **if** (1/3) — contract N, emit Y (aarch64_emit_if.oo exists), empty N. The if emit uses a position-return-on-mismatch pattern (a64_after_if_head at aarch64_emit_if.oo:16-22, a64_skip_braced at aarch64_emit_if.oo:43-63) rather than the `let empty: List[String] = list_new()` pattern. a64_if_cond at aarch64_emit_if.oo:138-176 returns "other" on shape mismatch, which the dispatcher treats as leftover and exits with `ERR aarch64_emit leftover println` (aarch64_emit.oo:122-125). The contract gate does not look for KW_IF.

The x86 audit had no 1/3 rows; aarch64's 1/3 set is the row where the literal-name rule is loosened by one cell.

---

## 4. Comparison: aarch64 vs x86

| #  | Feature    | x86 contract | aarch64 contract | x86 emit | aarch64 emit | Verdict   | Evidence                                                                                       |
|----|------------|--------------|------------------|----------|--------------|-----------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
| 1  | let binding| N            | N                | N        | N            | same (0/3)| x86_emit_typed_let.oo:16 vs aarch64_emit_take.oo:67                                            |
| 2  | fn decl    | Y            | Y                | Y        | N            | worse     | x86_emit_fn.oo:20 vs (no aarch64_emit_fn.oo)                                                    |
| 3  | match      | N            | N                | Y        | N            | worse     | x86_emit_match.oo:40 vs (no aarch64 file)                                                       |
| 4  | if         | N            | N                | N        | Y            | better*   | x86_emit_ctl.oo:19 vs aarch64_emit_if.oo:16                                                     |
| 5  | while      | N            | N                | N        | N            | same (0/3)| x86_emit_loop.oo:31 vs (no aarch64 file)                                                        |
| 6  | for        | N            | N                | Y        | N            | worse     | x86_emit_for.oo:20 vs (no aarch64 file)                                                         |
| 7  | struct     | N            | N                | Y        | N            | worse     | x86_emit_struct.oo:55 vs (no aarch64 file)                                                      |
| 8  | list       | N            | N                | Y        | N            | worse     | x86_emit_list.oo:29 vs (no aarch64 file)                                                        |
| 9  | opt        | N            | N                | Y        | N            | worse     | x86_emit_opt.oo:144 vs (no aarch64 file)                                                        |
| 10 | int        | N            | N                | Y        | N            | worse     | x86_emit_int.oo:16 vs aarch64_emit_num.oo:14 (helpers only)                                     |
| 11 | float      | N            | N                | Y        | N            | worse     | x86_emit_float.oo:91 vs (no aarch64 file)                                                       |
| 12 | string     | N            | N                | Y        | N            | worse     | x86_emit_str.oo:20 vs aarch64_emit_harv.oo:74 (helpers only)                                    |
| 13 | fn call    | N            | N                | N        | N            | same (0/3)| x86_emit_rsi.oo:20 vs aarch64_emit_take.oo:110 (partial 0-arg only)                            |
| 14 | return     | N            | N                | N        | N            | same (0/3)| x86_emit_ctl.oo:174 vs aarch64_emit_take.oo:17 (single-shape scan only)                        |
| 15 | rec fn     | N            | N                | Y        | N            | worse     | x86_emit_rec.oo:18 vs (no aarch64 file)                                                         |
| 16 | mut local  | N            | N                | Y        | N            | worse     | x86_emit_mut.oo:17 vs (no aarch64 file)                                                         |

Net: 0 features better, 1 better-with-asterisk, 10 worse, 5 same. The aarch64 path is shallower on the emit axis (only 1 of 16 features has a literal emit file) but matches the x86 contract coverage on every row.

The *if* row is the one aarch64 win: aarch64_emit_if.oo has a dedicated file, where x86 folds if into x86_emit_ctl.oo (a "closest, not literal" mapping). The asterisk is because aarch64_emit_if.oo uses position-return-on-mismatch (aarch64_emit_if.oo:16-22) instead of the `let empty` policy, so the contract-style "refuse-don't-miscompile" guarantee is weaker. Treat as a tie-leaning-better rather than a clean win.

The contract columns are identical between x86 and aarch64: both gates track KW_FN, KW_REQUIRES, KW_ENSURES, KW_VERIFY, and IDENT "spec" and ignore every other language feature (aarch64_emit_contract.oo:84, 91, 107, 120 mirrors x86_emit_contract.oo:82, 89, 105, 118). The Tier 0 #3 port closed the gate-existence gap but did not widen the gate.

---

## 5. Recommended additions to the aarch64 contract gate

One entry per §2 feature. Each addition names the token to detect inside aarch64_contract_walk_records (aarch64_emit_contract.oo:75) and the failure mode it would surface.

1. **let binding** — add a `KW_LET` arm after the `KW_FN` block at aarch64_emit_contract.oo:84. On detect, require the right-hand side to be one of the harvest-acceptable shapes: STRING, INT (with optional + - * / % INT), MINUS INT, KW_TRUE, KW_FALSE, or IDENT (`aarch64_emit_take.oo:76-159`). Otherwise emit `ERR aarch64_emit contract not folded: let <name>`. Today a non-const let silently fails the harvest and trips aarch64_emit.oo:122-125 (`ERR aarch64_emit leftover println`).
2. **match** — add a `KW_MATCH` arm. Require the scrutinee to be an IDENT bound in the same scope and each arm to be a literal pattern (`LITERAL`, `_`, or bare IDENT). Otherwise return `ERR aarch64_emit contract not folded: match <scrut>`. This catches every match expression today, since aarch64_emit.oo:22-127 has no match path.
3. **while** — add a `KW_WHILE` arm. Require the condition to be a const-foldable comparison and require a same-scope `KW_LET KW_MUT` init to exist. The harvest walker has no while path (aarch64_emit.oo only walks KW_IF, KW_LET, println, LBRACE, RBRACE), so the gate would surface every while as an early refuse.
4. **for** — add a `KW_FOR` arm. Require `IDENT KW_IN <range>` where `<range>` is `INT DOTDOT INT`. Anything else returns `ERR aarch64_emit contract not folded: for <var>`. No for-loop is compilable on aarch64 today.
5. **struct** — add a `KW_STRUCT` arm. Require the field list to be a non-empty `IDENT COLON <type>` sequence and require the type to be a known KW (Int, Float, String, Bool). Otherwise return `ERR aarch64_emit contract not folded: struct <name>`. Closes the silent-drop on every struct decl.
6. **list** — add a `KW_LIST` arm. Require the element type to be KW_INT, KW_FLOAT, KW_STRING, or KW_BOOL and require the element count to be a literal INT or DOTDOT-bound range. Otherwise return `ERR aarch64_emit contract not folded: list <elt>`. Catches every list literal today.
7. **opt** — add a `KW_OPT` arm. Require the inner type to be one of KW_INT, KW_FLOAT, KW_STRING, KW_BOOL, or a declared struct. Otherwise return `ERR aarch64_emit contract not folded: opt <inner>`. Today opt is not harvest-accepted.
8. **int** — add a `KW_INT` arm at the type-position (after a COLON). Require it to be paired with a const-foldable INT literal on the right. Otherwise return `ERR aarch64_emit contract not folded: int`. Pairs with aarch64_emit_num.oo:14-26.
9. **float** — add a `KW_FLOAT` arm. Same shape as int but for FLOAT. Otherwise return `ERR aarch64_emit contract not folded: float`. Today no aarch64 file emits floats.
10. **string** — add a `STRING` arm at the let-RHS position. Require the right-hand side to be a STRING or a STRING PLUS STRING chain (mirroring a64_str_fold at aarch64_emit_harv.oo:74-88). Otherwise return `ERR aarch64_emit contract not folded: string`. Pairs with the existing harvest.
11. **fn call** — add a `KW_FN` + `LPAREN` + `IDENT` + `RPAREN` detection inside the fn-body scan. If the call has a non-const-foldable argument list (more than 1 arg, struct arg, fn arg, list arg) return `ERR aarch64_emit contract not folded: call <name>`. Today the harvest only accepts 0-arg and 1-STRING-arg calls (aarch64_emit_take.oo:110-129, 218-231).
12. **return** — add a `KW_RETURN` arm. Require the returned expression to be a STRING literal, an INT literal, a MINUS INT, a bare IDENT bound in the same fn, or a 0-arg fn call. Anything else (struct field, list index, binary op, ternary) returns `ERR aarch64_emit contract not folded: return <expr>`. Today a multi-line return or a non-string return is silently miscompiled by a64_fn_ret_str (aarch64_emit_take.oo:17-34).
13. **rec fn** — add a `KW_FN` + `IDENT` + `{` scan that flags the same IDENT appearing as a callee inside the body. Tag the fn as `rec` and require the body to fold the same way a non-rec fn body would. Otherwise return `ERR aarch64_emit contract not folded: rec <name>`. The aarch64 path has no rec-fns path today.
14. **mut local** — add a `KW_MUT` arm at the let-binding position (between `KW_LET` and `IDENT`). Require the right-hand side to be one of the harvest-acceptable shapes AND require the IDENT to be re-assigned somewhere in the same scope (detect `IDENT EQ ...` after the let). Otherwise return `ERR aarch64_emit contract not folded: mut <name>`. Today mut is silently treated as immutable.

---

## 6. Top 3 weakest features with action plan

The fourteen §2 features all have 0/3 coverage. The three with the largest downstream blast radius on the aarch64 path are:

1. **fn call** — widest blast radius. The aarch64 path silently accepts 0-arg and 1-STRING-arg calls (aarch64_emit_take.oo:110-129, 218-231) and refuses everything else by exiting with `ERR aarch64_emit leftover println` (aarch64_emit.oo:122-125). A 2+arg call never reaches the harvest; the user gets a generic leftover error with no shape hint. Action: copy the a64_record_let 0-arg/1-arg fn-call shapes into a new aarch64_match_fn_call function in a new aarch64_emit_fn_call.oo; parameterize on arity 0..8 (AAPCS64 has 8 register args); gate on the contract clause from §5 #11. This single addition closes the 0/3 row and unblocks the multi-arg case before the harvest runs.
2. **if** — second-largest blast radius, but already at 1/3 (aarch64_emit_if.oo exists). The contract gate is the missing cell. Action: add the KW_IF clause from §5 #4 (folded into a single ref; see §5 #4 in this audit, not the x86 audit's #2) to aarch64_contract_walk_records at aarch64_emit_contract.oo:84; when the gate trips, the dispatch in aarch64_emit.oo:122-125 already prints `ERR aarch64_emit leftover println` and exits, so adding the gate front-stops the silent-miscompile path. Pair with converting aarch64_emit_if.oo's position-return-on-mismatch (aarch64_emit_if.oo:16-22, 43-63) to the `let empty: List[String] = list_new()` policy used in aarch64_emit_take.oo:68.
3. **let binding** — broad blast radius because let is the most common statement in user code. Today a non-const let silently fails the harvest and trips aarch64_emit.oo:122-125. Action: add the KW_LET clause from §5 #1 to aarch64_contract_walk_records; reuse a64_record_let (aarch64_emit_take.oo:67) as the sub-arm of a new generic aarch64_emit_let.oo; that file follows the x86_emit_<feature>.oo convention and lifts the empty policy from aarch64_emit_take.oo:68 to the new file's public entry point.

These three additions turn the 0/3 column into 3/3 for the most-hit features and close the gap to the x86 row's 10 × 2/3 coverage. The remaining 11 features can land in any order once these three are stable.
